#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Search for a run that completes a level, prove it, and score how hard it was.
#
#   bin/solve levels/13-reservoir.json
#   bin/solve levels/*.json --jobs 6 --time 300 --json /tmp/solved.json
#   bin/solve levels/08-needle.json --save /tmp/          # writes a replay file
#   bin/solve levels/08-needle.json --time 900 --effort 4        # try much harder
#   bin/solve levels/*.json --rev 5 --linear 1                   # the experimental rev
#   bin/solve levels/21-draft.json --progress                    # say what it is doing
#
#   bin/solve levels/21-rollers.json --optimise --time 1200      # find a FAST run
#   bin/solve levels/06-two-doors.json --optimise --tension      # what is the ORDER worth
#   bin/solve levels/21-rollers.json --optimise --order greedy   # force the obvious order
#   bin/solve levels/08-needle.json --human                      # ride it with hands
#
# --optimise CHANGES THE QUESTION. Without it this tool satisfices, on purpose
# and at all three levels of its decision: the first apple order that completes
# wins, the first strategy that gets a leg done wins, and the first arrival the
# search trips over is the one it hands on. That is exactly right for the thing
# the default is FOR — "can this level be done at all", run on every generated
# level, where one run is the whole answer and cheapness is the point.
#
# With it, the tool gates first — one cheap satisficing pass, exactly as above —
# and then spends the rest of --time beating what the gate found. Every round
# searches against a CEILING set to the best run so far, so a round either
# returns something strictly faster or returns nothing, and the answer can only
# improve. --gate-time caps the gate's share; the default is a quarter.
#
# IT ALSO CHANGES THE SCORE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF HAVING IT. Difficulty is
# measured on the line the tool ends up with, and the gate's line and the
# optimiser's line are different runs over the same rock. Re-measuring the same
# level on the faster one moves the composite by up to fourteen points, and on
# 21-rollers the FAST line is the ROOMIER one — head clearance 0.282 m against
# 0.586 m. So every score this tool prints says which line it came from, and
# every stored row carries the same stamp. A score that does not say how it was
# measured cannot be compared to another score.
#
# It is much more expensive and it is not the gate. Twelve to twenty minutes a
# level rather than two, and the number it produces is PAR — a competitive time,
# reported as its own line — rather than completability.
#
# --tension MEASURES WHAT THE APPLE ORDER IS WORTH. It solves a second time with
# plain nearest-apple-first forced and reports the spread between the two. That
# is DEPTH, not difficulty, so it is reported as its own axis and never folded
# into the score: a level where the obvious order is twenty-five seconds off the
# best one has something to discover, which is a different claim from "this is
# hard to ride". Judged against a ~3 s run-to-run noise floor — a smaller spread
# than that is not a finding. It doubles the cost, which is why it is a flag:
# the cheap gate runs on every generated level and has to stay cheap.
# --order greedy forces that order for a single run, if you want to watch it.
#
# --progress streams the search log as it happens instead of printing it in the
# final report. A single-level solve is otherwise silent for one to fourteen
# minutes, which is indistinguishable from a hung process — and it is what the
# level editor's deliberate feedback tier reads.
#
# WHICH PHYSICS. `--rev` defaults to SIM_REV, the LIVE rev, and NOT to the newest
# one that exists — a solver that quietly searched experimental physics would
# report a level completable that nobody playing the game can complete. `--linear`
# sets rev 5's rider.inertialTransferLinear; it is refused below rev 5 rather than
# silently ignored. The replay a solve saves is stamped with the rev it was found
# on, so bin/replay runs it on that rev's frozen engine.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. bin/level-drive is a scripted rider — throttle when a wheel
# is down, lean into the face — and the plan calls it "a lower bound, not a
# proof". It duly reported 13-reservoir, 15-feather and 17-backswing as
# uncompletable, which nobody believed, because the pass/fail pattern across
# levels looked like the pilot's brittleness rather than a physics wall. This is
# the real answer: a search over the deterministic sim, through `advance(mask)`
# so that anything it finds is expressible as a replay, and it either hands you
# a watchable run or tells you exactly where it got stuck.
#
# It produces five things and all five have downstream users:
#   completability   the gate for AI level generation and the 100-level target
#   a replay file    standard envelope, verified, `bin/replay` or L in the client
#   PAR              the fastest run anything has found, which is the one number
#                    here that is about the level and nothing else, and the one
#                    the level chooser shows beside your own best
#   a difficulty score  measured on that line, and stamped with which line
#   route tension    what the apple order is worth, under --tension
#
# THE SCORE HAS TWO AXES AND THEY BOTH DESCRIBE THE LEVEL. `slip` is how often a
# human-sized fumble on the measured line kills you; `clearance` is how close the
# rider's head gets to rock on it. Expansions per route metre and deaths per
# expansion are still measured and still printed — as `effort` and `lethality`,
# under "diagnostics" — and neither is in the score any more, because both
# measure the SEARCH. 17-backswing scored high on effort because the occupancy
# grid routes up a wall, which is a fact about src/solver/field.ts. When they are
# reported they are still scored on the GATE's pass, so that they stay comparable
# between levels instead of reporting how long the optimiser was given.
#
# UNRATED IS A STATE, NOT A CRASH. A level the search cannot complete is reported
# with a reason code rather than a number: `unsolved` (more budget might do it —
# this tool has been wrong in that direction twice), `zero-apples` (see below), or
# `unverified` (a run was found and did not re-simulate, which is a bug). The
# chooser renders the state; it never invents a score.
#
# A LEVEL WITH NO APPLES IS A KNOWN HOLE AND IT SAYS SO. With no apples the whole
# solve collapses to one leg, start to flower, steered by the geodesic distance to
# the flower — and 26-nothing-to-take is a level where reaching the flower means
# riding AWAY from it first, so every state the search would have to pass through
# looks worse than the one before it and a weighted A* does not go there. It is
# provably completable: an input stream driven on a copy with a helper apple in
# the pit finishes the SHIPPED file in 11.61 s. Gating the format needs a
# WAYPOINT, not more budget — a position the run has to pass through, the way an
# apple is. The existing segment thresholds are not one: they chop a leg by
# DISTANCE TO THE TARGET, which is satisfied just as well from the wrong side of
# the pit, and that is exactly where this level strands the search (it stalls
# 35 m past the flower, having ridden the grid's optimistic route over the gap).
#
# COST. This is not a CI tool. The two tutorial levels take two and four
# seconds; a typical level takes one to four minutes on one core and the worst
# of the twenty takes fourteen. The whole set is about an hour of CPU, which is
# ~22 minutes of wall clock at --jobs 7, and peaks around 600 MB of nodes on a
# hard level. --time caps each level; a level that hits the cap is reported
# UNSOLVED with the point it stalled at, which is an honest "could not" and
# never a "cannot" — the solver has been wrong in that direction twice already.
#
# Exit status: 0 every level solved, 1 at least one did not, 2 bad usage.
source "$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/_common.sh"
ensure_deps
cd "$ROOT"
[[ $# -ge 1 ]] || { echo "usage: bin/solve <level.json> [more.json ...] [--save <dir|file>] [--jobs N] [--time SECONDS] [--optimise [--gate-time SECONDS] [--tension] [--order greedy|i,j,k]]" >&2; exit 2; }
exec "$ROOT/node_modules/.bin/tsx" "$ROOT/bin/_solve.ts" "$@"
